[interviewer · INTRO] Your client is Brew & Bean, a specialty coffee chain with 200 locations across the US. Over the past two years, their net profit margin has declined from 24% to 6%, even though revenues have grown 15% over the same period. The CEO has hired us to identify the root cause of this profit decline and recommend how to reverse it. How would you approach this problem?

Take a minute if you need it, then tell me how you'd structure your approach.

[candidate silent 25s]

[candidate · 1:55] Okay, so the headline is revenue up 15% but margin collapsing from 24% to 6% — that tells me this is almost certainly a cost problem, not a demand problem, though I want to verify that revenue growth isn't coming at a bad price.

I'd structure it as profit = revenue minus costs, and go down both sides.

On revenue: is the 15% growth from more stores, more transactions per store, or higher ticket? New stores could be dragging margin if they're immature.

On costs: split fixed versus variable. Variable would be coffee beans, milk, cups, store labor. Fixed would be rent, corporate overhead, marketing, equipment.

Then I'd benchmark each cost line as a percent of revenue, two years ago versus today, and find which lines expanded.

Before I dig in — do we have menu price history? I'd like to know whether they've raised prices at all over the two years.
