[interviewer · EXHIBIT · 3:31] I'll apply one pressure test to your structure first, then you'll get data. Your framework splits costs into variable and fixed and revenue into stores, transactions, and ticket. You asked about that — 200 stores today — and it was 200 stores two years ago, so the footprint hasn’t changed. COGS is 58% of revenue today, up from 42% two years ago. Hold that request; I'll come back to it shortly. Which branch would you prioritize first, and why?

[candidate · 5:01] COGS, clearly — and I'd prioritize it hard.

Here's the logic. Footprint is flat at 200 stores, so the 15% revenue growth is same-store volume. Prices haven't moved, so that growth is all transactions or mix, not price. That means revenue per unit is unchanged while COGS per dollar of revenue went from 42% to 58% — a 16 point swing.

To size it: if the total margin decline is 18 points, from 24% to 6%, COGS alone explains 16 of those 18. That's roughly 90% of the problem sitting in one line. Everything else — rent, labor, overhead — accounts for maybe 2 points combined. Not where I'd spend my time.

And because prices are frozen, there's no price offset absorbing the input inflation. The whole increase drops straight to the bottom line.

So inside COGS, I'd want the split: beans, dairy, cups and packaging, food. Do we have that?
