[interviewer · BRAINSTORM · 5:06] The labor and overhead requests are now earned too, since they've asserted those lines account for little. Coffee beans were 25% of COGS two years ago. Raw coffee bean costs are up 40% over the past two years. Other input costs — dairy, packaging, and food — are up 37.5% over the past two years. Labor is 22% of revenue, and it’s been stable. Overhead is 14% of revenue, up slightly from 12%.

[candidate · 6:36] Let me run the COGS math.

Two years ago COGS was 42% of revenue. Beans at 25% of that = 10.5% of revenue. Everything else — dairy, packaging, food — is 75% = 31.5% of revenue.

Now apply the inflation. Beans up 40%: 10.5 × 1.4 = 14.7% of revenue. Other inputs up 37.5%: 31.5 × 1.375 = 43.3%. Add those: 58.0% of revenue. That ties out exactly to the 58% you gave me, which is a good check — and it only ties because prices are flat, so cost as a percent of revenue moves purely with input prices.

Now the interesting part. Beans added 4.2 points of margin. Other inputs added 11.8 points. So despite the 40% headline on coffee, beans are not the story — dairy, packaging and food are nearly three times the damage, because they're three-quarters of the basket.

Overhead adds 2 more points. 16 plus 2 is 18 — the full decline, fully explained.
