[interviewer · RECOMMENDATION · 6:38] Okay. The cost drivers are now quantified, so I'll ask the open question. Beyond what we've discussed, what else could Brew & Bean do to reverse this decline?

[candidate · 8:08] The biggest untouched lever is price. Prices have been flat two years while inputs rose ~38%. If costs rise and price doesn't, margin has to absorb all of it — that's mechanically what happened.

Quick sizing: a 10% price increase on flat volume takes COGS from 58% to about 52.7% of revenue, labor from 22% to 20%, overhead 14% to 12.7%. That's roughly 8.5 points of margin back, getting them to around 14-15%. Even if we lose some volume, specialty coffee is fairly inelastic and they've trained customers to expect no increases — there's headroom.

Beyond price: renegotiate dairy and packaging contracts, since that's where 11.8 of the 16 points sit — consolidate suppliers across 200 stores for volume leverage. Hedge or forward-contract beans. Reformulate or right-size portions. Push mix toward higher-margin items — food attach, larger sizes.

Also worth checking overhead, the 2-point creep.

Risk: raising price after two years flat may feel abrupt — I'd phase it, test in a few markets first.
