[interviewer · RECOMMENDATION · 8:11] Walk me through the 8.5 points. How did you get from a 10% price increase to that figure?

[candidate · 9:41] Sure. Start from today's P&L as a percent of revenue: COGS 58, labor 22, overhead 14. That's 94, leaving the 6% margin. Ties out.

Now raise price 10% with volume held flat. Revenue goes from 100 to 110. None of those cost lines move in dollar terms — I'm selling the same number of cups, same beans, same milk, same staff, same rent. So the dollars stay at 58, 22, and 14, but I'm dividing by 110 instead of 100.

COGS: 58/110 = 52.7%. Labor: 22/110 = 20.0%. Overhead: 14/110 = 12.7%. Total 85.4%, so margin is 14.6% — call it 14.5.

From 6% to 14.5% is 8.5 points. Another way to see it: the 10 points of new revenue are pure incremental margin, and 10/110 = 9.1 points, less a rounding bit.

Key caveat — that's zero volume loss. If I lose 10% of transactions, revenue is flat and I've gained nothing.
