[interviewer · RECOMMENDATION · 9:43] Understood. You've said specialty coffee is fairly inelastic and that customers have been trained to expect no increases. A skeptical CEO says raising prices will drive customers to competitors and the plan is too risky. How do you respond?

[candidate · 11:13] Fair challenge, and I'd meet it with the breakeven math rather than an assertion.

At today's 58% COGS, each dollar of sales carries about 42 cents of variable contribution before labor and overhead. If I raise price 10%, I can afford to lose a meaningful share of transactions before I'm worse off — roughly, I'd need to lose more than about 19% of volume for the price increase to be net negative on contribution. That's a big drop. Specialty coffee is a habit purchase with high visit frequency and real switching friction — location, app, loyalty, routine.

I'd also point out doing nothing is not the safe option. Inputs have risen 38% in two years with no sign of reversing; at 6% margin, another few points of input inflation puts them underwater.

And I'd de-risk it: phase the increase, 5% then 5%; test in two or three markets first; measure transaction counts weekly against control markets; lead with higher-ticket items rather than the core drip cup.
