[interviewer · RECOMMENDATION · 11:16] Walk me through the 19% breakeven figure. What contribution did you use, and how did you get to that volume loss?

[candidate · 12:46] I used COGS as the only variable cost, so contribution margin is 100 minus 58 = 42%. Per unit, think of price as $1.00 and variable cost as $0.58, giving $0.42 of contribution.

Raise price 10%: price is now $1.10, variable cost is unchanged at $0.58 because the cup costs what it costs. New contribution is $0.52 per unit.

Breakeven is where total contribution is unchanged. Old total was 0.42 × Q. New is 0.52 × Q_new. So Q_new/Q = 0.42/0.52 = 0.808. That's a 19.2% allowable volume decline.

One caveat on my own number: I treated labor as fixed, which is defensible in the short run — you're staffing the store regardless — but if labor flexes with volume, true variable cost is higher, contribution is lower, and the breakeven cushion shrinks. I'd want to know how much of that 22% is schedulable hours before I'd commit to 19%.
